WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.300
IPC can accidentally expose more than the intended request or response.

2
00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:15.560
Messages may contain secrets, tokens, file paths, descriptors, handles, configuration values, internal identifiers, or state snapshots.

3
00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:21.100
Even when the channel is local, that data should be protected according to the trust level of the peers.

4
00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:28.820
Child processes may inherit descriptors, handles, environment values, or other process state unless launch behavior is controlled.

5
00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:35.800
A helper process that only needs one pipe may receive access to other IPC channels if the parent does not manage inheritance.

6
00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:39.020
That can make the boundary between processes less clear.

7
00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:41.940
Sensitive messages should be minimized.

8
00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:48.600
If a peer only needs a reference or status value, avoid sending large internal structures or secrets.

9
00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:54.380
Logs and diagnostics should avoid dumping IPC payloads or internal authorization details.

10
00:00:54.480 --> 00:01:01.120
Good troubleshooting output identifies the failed operation without exposing the data that the IPC channel was meant to protect.

11
00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:08.360
Passing handles or descriptors requires clear ownership, lifetime, and permission expectations.

12
00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:09.820
Who may use the handle?

13
00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:11.040
Who closes it?

14
00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:14.900
Does receiving it imply authorization for the underlying object?

15
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:16.680
Can it outlive the request?

16
00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:19.820
These questions should be part of the protocol design.

17
00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:27.300
Descriptor and handle hygiene should be reviewed wherever processes are launched, privileges change, or helpers are invoked.

18
00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:35.560
The review should confirm what is inherited, what is closed, what is intentionally passed, and how sensitive values are kept out of places that do not need them.
